module gunbc.host.boot_attempt

// THE NEUTRAL BOOT-ATTEMPT RECORDS (managed-host untangle, cut 4d-ii; operator ruling 2026-10-14:
// one physical machine boot transaction, physical and guest control remain distinct). This module is
// the LOWER half of the boot attempt: the records every machine's boot produces, described by facts
// that do not depend on which controller protocol (MegaRAC/IPMI/Redfish) read them. Adapters depend
// on the core, never the core on an adapter: the controller-specific readings (SEL, SDR, sensors,
// inventory, param5, chassis power, the Redfish URLs) and the MegaRAC-shaped diagnostic live in the
// controller's adapter module (gunbc.machine_intake.megarac_boot_diagnostic_bundle), which imports
// THIS module. What neutrality holds here is per-DECLARATION (review 85832): NO TYPE IN THIS MODULE NAMES A CONTROLLER PROTOCOL: no type is extdeps.bmc.ipmi*, no
// megarac_*, and the run record carries the adapter's diagnostic BY REFERENCE (the retained artifact
// path plus its digest, and the neutral findings), not by the adapter's reading type.
//
// THE BOUNDARY LEAK, DISCHARGED (review 85832; §4d-ii, node://adhoc-18598508-4f4). The
// paragraph this slot carried said the core was protocol-free per-DECLARATION but not
// per-CLOSURE: it borrowed six console declarations out of two machine-named modules
// (SpanConsoleReading and span_console_readings from
// gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_power_on_account; ConsoleMilestoneObservation,
// mtcollins1_console_milestones and console_milestone_sighting_text from
// gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_console_milestone_observation), and those imports dragged
// the machines' SEL/SDR/memory parsers into the core's closure. That is no longer true: the
// re-home landed. Every console declaration this module used to declare or borrow now lives
// at gunbc.host.boot_console_observation (the capture, ConsoleReading and its readers, the
// span pair taking already-split spans, and the whole console milestone module), and this
// module imports NOTHING console-shaped -- the closure is clean, per-declaration and
// per-module. The debt ended DISCHARGED: the six items on the typed carrier below name the
// RETIRED homes, the census lacks every one, and boot_attempt_console_debt_status reads
// DissolutionFired (std.dissolution retires_dissolution; DESIGN §5). A row that wants its
// console reading imports it from the neutral home.

import std.types { Bool, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.measure { Second, second }
import std.algebra { FreeSemigroup }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import std.dissolution { DissolutionCondition, DissolutionStatus, dissolution_status, retires_dissolution }
import extdeps.tools.sha256sum { Sha256FileDigest, Sha256FileDigestUnavailable }
import gunbc.host.managed_host_boot_phase_timing { BootPhaseTiming }

// ── THE NEUTRAL POWER STATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The on/off the boot observes, protocol-agnostic: the controller's raw reading (MegaRAC's
// ChassisPowerObservation, a future controller's own) maps to this. Unread carries why, so a state the
// boot could not establish says which read failed. (Correction 1: minted on proven absence.)

type HostPowerState
  = PowerOn
  | PowerOff
  | PowerUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn host_power_text(state: HostPowerState) -> String {
  match state {
    PowerOn => "on"
    PowerOff => "off"
    PowerUnread { reason: r } => concat("unobserved: ", r as String)
  }
}

// THE MEDIA PRESENTATION STATE, protocol-agnostic: is the medium the attempt wants actually being
// served, and if not, in which of three different ways not. The three are NOT interchangeable and the
// old two-arm shape collapsed two of them:
//
//   Presented                    — the controller says the medium is being served.
//   PresentationSessionHeld      — it was served, but only while OUR attach session lived, and that
//                                  session is now closed. A presentation that dies with the session is
//                                  not a presentation the boot will find; it is also not a failed read.
//   PresentationNotServed        — the controller answered, and what it answered is that the medium is
//                                  not being served, with its reason. "Not attempted" used to be this
//                                  arm's name, which read as "nobody looked" for a look that was made
//                                  and came back negative.
//   PresentationUnread           — the read itself did not answer. Indeterminate: neither served nor
//                                  not served is known.
//
// The adapter classifies an ATTACH result and a read-only LOOK with two different functions
// (megarac_presentation / megarac_look_presentation). They must not share one: an attach result and a
// look are different observations, and classifying a look with the attach classifier reports every
// healthy look as "not attempted", because a look is by construction not an attach.

type BootPresentation
  = Presented
  | PresentationSessionHeld { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | PresentationNotServed { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | PresentationUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// THE ONE RENDERING of a presentation state, beside host_power_text for the same reason: the media
// attach line, the handoff after-look line and the end-of-attempt look line all describe this one
// concept, and an arm added here shows up in all three at once. Anywhere else that needs these words
// asks for them; a fourth match on BootPresentation outside these two fns is the drift this prevents.
fn presentation_text(p: BootPresentation) -> String {
  match p {
    Presented => "presented"
    PresentationSessionHeld { detail: d } => concat("served only under our attach session: ", d as String)
    PresentationNotServed { reason: r } => concat("not served: ", r as String)
    PresentationUnread { reason: r } => concat("unread: ", r as String)
  }
}

// ── THE MEDIA ATTACH, AS THIS ATTEMPT'S RECORD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The handoff is gated on this record, and the run record carries the same record (side chat on
// gunbc#12425). What the boot was gated on — which intended medium and its verified digest, the
// redirection wire, when — reaches the actuation, the attempt and this record as a value, including
// the refusal and timeout arms, never as a bare exit.
//
// NEUTRAL (managed-host untangle, cut 4d-ii): the raw MegaRAC evidence (the attempt's transport log,
// the chassis power reading, the MegaRacAttachResult) lives in the adapter; this record keeps the
// classification — which medium (name + verified digest), the power state around the attach, whether
// a stale medium was powered off first, and the presentation state after. `ready` is NOT a stored
// field (correction 2): it is derivable from the record's own presentation field, so it is a fn.

type StaleMediumPowerOff
  = StalePowerOffNotNeeded { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | StalePowerOffObserved { stale_image: String, before: HostPowerState }
  | StalePowerOffCommandRefused { stale_image: String, before: HostPowerState }
  | StalePowerOffNotObserved { stale_image: String, before: HostPowerState, last: HostPowerState }

fn stale_power_off_text(record: StaleMediumPowerOff) -> String {
  match record {
    StalePowerOffNotNeeded { reason: r } => concat("no power-off: ", r as String)
    StalePowerOffObserved { stale_image: i, before: b } => join(["powered off (was ", host_power_text(state: b), ") to replace stale '", i, "', observed off"], "")
    StalePowerOffCommandRefused { stale_image: i, before: _ } => join(["chassis power off for stale '", i, "' was refused by the controller"], "")
    StalePowerOffNotObserved { stale_image: i, before: _, last: l } => join(["chassis power off for stale '", i, "' was sent but never observed off; last read ", host_power_text(state: l)], "")
  }
}

type MediaAttachRecord
  = MediaAttachNotAttempted { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | MediaAttachAttempted {
      medium_image_name: NonEmptyStr,
      medium_image_sha256: NonEmptyStr,
      power: HostPowerState,
      stale_power_off: StaleMediumPowerOff,
      presentation: BootPresentation
    }
fn media_attach_text(record: MediaAttachRecord) -> String {
  match record {
    MediaAttachNotAttempted { reason: r } => concat("not attempted: ", r as String)
    MediaAttachAttempted { medium_image_name: n, medium_image_sha256: h, power: p, stale_power_off: s, presentation: pr } =>
      join([
        "'", n, "' (sha256: ", h, ") ",
        match p {
          PowerOff => "attached with the host off"
          PowerOn => "attached (host was on)"
          PowerUnread { reason: rr } => join(["attached (host was unobserved: ", rr as String, ")"], "")
        },
        "; ", (stale_power_off_text(record: s) as String),
        "; presentation: ",
        presentation_text(p: pr)
      ], "")
  }
}

// THE HANDOFF. The boot hands off to the OS, then reads back the presentation to confirm the medium
// the OS is actually booting from is the one it attached. The adapter's raw readback is its fact; this
// record keeps the neutral outcome (confirmed / attempted-unconfirmed / refused) and the presentation
// state after.

// Two arms, not three. A third, "the controller refused the handoff", was here and was deleted: the
// raw record the adapter stores is built from the handoff COMMAND's exit (exit 0 => confirmed, a
// failure => attempted-but-unconfirmed), so no observation can ever produce a refusal, the classifier
// that is the only writer has no arm for one, and the harness never raises one. An arm nothing can
// construct is not safety, it is a word in the vocabulary that means nothing.
type HandoffCallResult
  = HandoffConfirmed
  | HandoffAttemptedUnconfirmed { reason: NonEmptyStr }

type HandoffMedia
  = HandoffMediaNotReached { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | HandoffMediaWithheldAtRecheck { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | HandoffMediaAttempted {
      handoff: HandoffCallResult,
      after: BootPresentation
    }

fn handoff_media_text(record: HandoffMedia) -> String {
  match record {
    HandoffMediaNotReached { reason: r } => concat("not reached: ", r as String)
    HandoffMediaWithheldAtRecheck { reason: r } => concat("withheld at re-check: ", r as String)
    HandoffMediaAttempted { handoff: h, after: a } =>
      join(["attempted: ",
        match h {
          HandoffConfirmed => "confirmed"
          HandoffAttemptedUnconfirmed { reason: rr } => join(["attempted, unconfirmed: ", rr as String], "")
        },
        "; presentation after: ",
        presentation_text(p: a)], "")
  }
}

// THE END MEDIA. The final presentation look, taken after the OS has come up, as its own record. The
// adapter's raw read is its fact; this keeps the neutral presentation state.

type EndMedia
  = EndMediaNotTaken { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | EndMediaObserved { look: BootPresentation }

fn end_media_text(record: EndMedia) -> String {
  match record {
    EndMediaNotTaken { reason: r } => concat("not taken: ", r as String)
    EndMediaObserved { look: l } =>
      // One prefix over the ONE presentation rendering. The end look and the handoff after-look are
      // the same kind of fact read at a different moment, so they render identically apart from which
      // moment the line names; a fifth presentation arm is one edit in presentation_text, not three.
      concat("end look ", presentation_text(p: l))
  }
}

// ── THE POWER AFTER THE ATTEMPT, TERMINAL-NEUTRAL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (Correction 3.) The terminal is the consumer's, not the boot's: the diagnostic census's END marker
// is the census consumer's fact. The boot records the power state it observed and the terminal marker
// it saw (if any), and classifies the power against it. Whether that terminal was the census's END is
// the census consumer's mapping (it reads its own census END marker). PoweredOffAfterTerminal /
// PoweredOffWithoutTerminal replace the census-specific arm names.

type TerminalObserved
  = TerminalMarker { marker: NonEmptyStr }
  | TerminalNotObserved

fn terminal_text(t: TerminalObserved) -> String {
  match t {
    TerminalMarker { marker: m } => join(["terminal observed: '", m, "'"], "")
    TerminalNotObserved => "no terminal observed"
  }
}

type PowerAfterAttempt
  = PoweredOffAfterTerminal
  | PoweredOffWithoutTerminal
  | PoweredOnAfterAttempt
  | PowerAfterUnobserved { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | PowerAfterNotTaken { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// THE CLASSIFICATION, from the power state and whether a terminal marker was observed. The census
// consumer passes its own census END marker (or TerminalNotObserved if the marker was not seen); the
// boot does not know the census exists.

// THE READING, BEFORE THE PAIRING. A consumer that never made the read at all (no account modeled to
// read with, no time left in the budget) must say so, and that is not the same fact as a read that
// failed: HostPowerState describes what a read ANSWERED, so "not taken" belongs on the reading, not on
// an arm of PowerAfterAttempt. Without this carrier the adapter kept its own MegaRacPowerAfter sum to
// state it -- a second home for the pairing (review 81601).
type HostPowerReading
  = HostPowerRead { state: HostPowerState }
  | HostPowerNotTaken { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn power_after_attempt(power: HostPowerReading, terminal: TerminalObserved) -> PowerAfterAttempt {
  match power {
    HostPowerNotTaken { reason: r } => PowerAfterNotTaken { reason: r }
    HostPowerRead { state: s } =>
      match s {
        PowerOn => PoweredOnAfterAttempt
        PowerOff =>
          match terminal {
            TerminalMarker { marker: _ } => PoweredOffAfterTerminal
            TerminalNotObserved => PoweredOffWithoutTerminal
          }
        PowerUnread { reason: r } => PowerAfterUnobserved { reason: r }
      }
  }
}

fn power_after_text(p: PowerAfterAttempt) -> String {
  match p {
    PoweredOffAfterTerminal => "off after the observed terminal marker (expected: the attempt's image ends with a power-off)"
    PoweredOffWithoutTerminal => "off without an observed terminal marker"
    PoweredOnAfterAttempt => "on"
    PowerAfterUnobserved { reason: r } => concat("unobserved: ", r as String)
    PowerAfterNotTaken { reason: r } => concat("not taken: ", r as String)
  }
}

// ── THE BOOT OVERRIDE CONSUMPTION, NEUTRAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (Correction 6.) The input is the pending-before and pending-after classification, not the raw IPMI
// param5 read: the controller's adapter reads its param5 and classifies pending/not-pending, and the
// core derives the consumption from the two Booleans plus whether the boot went through the override's
// device. No IPMI type crosses into this module.

type BootOverrideConsumption
  = OverrideConsumedByAttempt
  | OverrideNoLongerPendingAfterAttempt
  | OverridePendingBeforeAttempt
  | OverrideStillPendingAfterAttempt
  | OverrideNotHandedOff { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | OverrideConsumptionUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// WHAT ONE PARAMETER-5 READ CAN SAY, before anything is decided about consumption. Three states, not
// two: the flag matched this attempt's override, it did not, or the read never answered -- and the
// last one carries WHY, which is the half a Bool loses. A consumer that reads the controller reduces
// its own reading to this and hands it here; that reduction is the adapter's job, the decision below is
// not its to make (review 81601: this decision had a second copy in the adapter, and production called
// that copy).
type BootOverrideStanding
  = OverrideFlagPending
  | OverrideFlagNotPending
  | OverrideReadUnanswered { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn boot_override_consumption(before: BootOverrideStanding, after: BootOverrideStanding, booted_through_override_device: Bool) -> BootOverrideConsumption {
  match before {
    OverrideReadUnanswered { reason: r } => OverrideConsumptionUnread { reason: r }
    OverrideFlagPending => OverridePendingBeforeAttempt
    OverrideFlagNotPending =>
      match after {
        OverrideReadUnanswered { reason: r } => OverrideConsumptionUnread { reason: r }
        OverrideFlagPending => OverrideStillPendingAfterAttempt
        OverrideFlagNotPending =>
          if booted_through_override_device {
            OverrideConsumedByAttempt
          } else {
            OverrideNoLongerPendingAfterAttempt
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE CLEARS AROUND THE SET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT THE ROUTE OWES THE PARAMETER IT SETS (merry-otter-837 ruling msg_36b81708). A pending override
// that predates the attempt can NEVER have its consumption attributed to the attempt -- the bytes stood
// there before the attempt wrote anything -- and that is the whole reason the before-read exists. So the
// pre-power step CLEARS what it finds pending, reads the clear back, and only then sets its own; and
// EVERY exit path after a set clears what it set, with a readback, including the refusals and the stops
// that never reached a console. An attempt that ends with the flag pending hands its own override to the
// next attempt: boot 5 run 40258937016 read 'Boot parameter data: a014000000' byte-identical before AND
// after its set and refused with 'a boot override matching this attempt's was already pending before it
// was set'; boot 6 run 40264233543 ended pending the same way. Protocol-neutral: a controller's adapter
// reduces its own read to these standings and keeps the bytes -- this module names no protocol.

// WHAT AN EXIT-PATH CLEAR OF THIS ATTEMPT'S OWN OVERRIDE SAID.
type BootOverrideClear
  = OverrideClearNotRequired { reason: NonEmptyStr }   // no set was ever issued: this attempt owns nothing to clear
  | OverrideCleared                                    // the readback said the override is gone
  | OverrideClearLeftPending                           // the readback STILL showed it pending: the next attempt inherits this one's
  | OverrideClearUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }        // the clear or its readback never answered

// HOW THIS ROUTE HANDLED AN OVERRIDE THAT WAS ALREADY PENDING BEFORE ITS OWN SET. There is deliberately
// NO Noop arm: 'the pending bytes already match this attempt's' is not a reason to skip the clear, it is
// the reason not to trust either read at all.
type StaleOverrideClearance
  = StaleOverrideNoneFound                        // the before-read answered, and nothing was pending
  | StaleOverrideCleared                          // pending found, cleared, and the readback said so
  | StaleOverrideWouldNotClear                    // cleared, and the readback still showed it pending: the attempt refuses
  | StaleOverrideUnread { reason: NonEmptyStr }   // the clear or its readback never answered

fn boot_override_clear_text(c: BootOverrideClear) -> String {
  match c {
    OverrideClearNotRequired { reason: r } => concat("not required: ", r as String)
    OverrideCleared => "cleared"
    OverrideClearLeftPending => "left pending"
    OverrideClearUnread { reason: r } => concat("unread: ", r as String)
  }
}

fn stale_override_clearance_text(c: StaleOverrideClearance) -> String {
  match c {
    StaleOverrideNoneFound => "none found"
    StaleOverrideCleared => "stale override cleared"
    StaleOverrideWouldNotClear => "would not clear"
    StaleOverrideUnread { reason: r } => concat("unread: ", r as String)
  }
}

// WHAT THE BEFORE-READ MEANS FOR THE CONSUMPTION JUDGMENT, once the pre-power clear has run. A clear
// that LANDED replaces the pending it found: what stood when this attempt's set went in is what the
// clear's OWN readback said, not what the pre-clear read said. Left uncorrected, a stale override turns
// every later attempt into OverridePendingBeforeAttempt -- and that arm returns before the after-read is
// ever inspected, so the after-read becomes dead information (clever-bat-328's finding).
// Clear-then-re-set is what makes the after-read informative again.
fn boot_override_standing_after_clear(before: BootOverrideStanding, clearance: StaleOverrideClearance) -> BootOverrideStanding {
  match clearance {
    StaleOverrideNoneFound => before
    StaleOverrideCleared => OverrideFlagNotPending
    StaleOverrideWouldNotClear => OverrideFlagPending
    StaleOverrideUnread { reason: r } => OverrideReadUnanswered { reason: concat("the stale override's clear never answered: ", r as String) }
  }
}

fn override_consumption_text(c: BootOverrideConsumption) -> String {
  match c {
    OverrideConsumedByAttempt => "consumed by this attempt: cleared after a terminal marker, which only a boot of the override's device prints"
    OverrideNoLongerPendingAfterAttempt => "no longer pending after the attempt, but no boot through the override's device was observed, so consumption by this attempt is not established"
    OverridePendingBeforeAttempt => "ALREADY PENDING before this attempt set it; consumption not attributable"
    OverrideStillPendingAfterAttempt => "STILL PENDING after the attempt; not consumed"
    OverrideNotHandedOff { reason: r } => concat("not handed off: ", r as String)
    OverrideConsumptionUnread { reason: r } => concat("undetermined: ", r as String)
  }
}

// ── THE ATTEMPT IDENTITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The attempt's own identity (run id), or why it could not be read.

type AttemptIdentityReading
  = AttemptRunId { run_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | AttemptIdentityUnavailable { variable: NonEmptyStr, detail: String }

fn attempt_identity_text(a: AttemptIdentityReading) -> String {
  match a {
    AttemptRunId { run_id: r } => r as String
    AttemptIdentityUnavailable { variable: v, detail: d } => concat("UNAVAILABLE: ", v as String, " could not be read: ", d)
  }
}

// ── THE ADAPTER'S DIAGNOSTIC, BY REFERENCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The by-reference pointer (operator ruling 2026-10-14). The run record does not carry the adapter's
// diagnostic by its MegaRAC type; it names where the adapter retained it (a path under the attempt's
// retained-artifact prefix) and the neutral findings. The adapter reads its own diagnostic back from
// that path.

type DiagnosticRef
  = DiagnosticRetained {
      path: NonEmptyStr,
      digest: Sha256FileDigest
    }
  | DiagnosticNotRetained { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | DiagnosticUnavailable { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn diagnostic_ref_text(d: DiagnosticRef) -> String {
  match d {
    // The digest is part of the reference -- it is how a consumer proves the file at that path is the
    // bytes this attempt captured -- so the renderer names it rather than dropping it.
    DiagnosticRetained { path: p, digest: dg } => match dg {
      Sha256FileDigest { digest: x } => concat(concat("retained at ", p), concat(" (sha256 ", x.hex as String))
      Sha256FileDigestUnavailable { path: up, reason: r } =>
        concat(concat("retained at ", p), concat(" (digest unavailable for ", concat(up, concat(": ", r))))
    }
    DiagnosticNotRetained { reason: r } => concat("not retained: ", r as String)
    DiagnosticUnavailable { reason: r } => concat("unavailable: ", r as String)
  }
}

// ── THE NEUTRAL FINDINGS, IN TWO KINDS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (correction 4: no stringly catch-all; every arm is a named, typed fact about the attempt. Findings
// the boot could not type — a controller-specific read failure, a raw reading — stay in the adapter's
// findings, not here.)
//
// Two kinds, because they have two different sources and DESIGN §3 wants each fact in exactly one:
//
//   StandingFinding      — a fact the run record's own fields CANNOT state, because it was observed
//                          beside the attempt rather than in it: which BMC channels failed, and whether
//                          the per-attempt credential was removed. Only a caller can supply these, so
//                          they are STORED on the record.
//   Derived               — a classification OF a field the record already holds: an attach that is not
//                          ready is what `media` says, a handoff that was refused is what `handoff_media`
//                          says, an override still pending is what `override` says. These are never
//                          stored. `run_record_findings` derives them, so the answer cannot disagree
//                          with the fields it is read from and cannot be stated twice. An earlier
//                          revision stored ONE `findings` list beside those fields and APPENDED these
//                          same classifications to it, which let a caller report a fact twice or report
//                          one its own fields contradict — review 81154, the same class of defect as
//                          correction 2's removal of the stored `ready`.
//


// A fact observed BESIDE the attempt, that none of its fields can state.
//
// EveryReadFailed carries the failed channel NAMES and nothing else. It used to carry a `count: Int`
// beside them, which was the same fact with two writers: a value could say count: 3 while naming one
// channel, and the only thing that could have caught it was a witness asserting the two agreed --
// validation standing in for construction, which §5 rules out ("a check restating a constraint the model
// already carries is a second representation of it") and which this module already refuses for the
// stored `ready` (correction 2). count is `length(channels)`; anyone who needs the number asks the list.
//
// `channels` is a FreeSemigroup, not a List (§3b Cardinality: nonemptiness belongs on
// std.algebra::FreeSemigroup). "Every read failed" with no channel named is not a fact anyone could act
// on, yet List permitted it; here the head exists by construction, so there is no empty value to refuse.
type StandingFinding
  = EveryReadFailed { channels: FreeSemigroup<NonEmptyStr> }
  | CredentialMayRemain

type DerivedFinding
  = MediaAttachNotReady { record: MediaAttachRecord }
  | MediaAttachSessionHeld { record: MediaAttachRecord }
  | HandoffWithheldAtRecheck { handoff: HandoffMedia }
  | HandoffUnconfirmed { handoff: HandoffMedia }
  | OverrideNotConsumed { consumption: BootOverrideConsumption }
  | OverrideStaleNotCleared { clearance: StaleOverrideClearance }
  | OverrideLeftPendingAtRelease { clear: BootOverrideClear }
  | PoweredOffWithoutTerminalMarker
  | PresentationAfterHandoff { presentation: BootPresentation }

// THE DERIVED FINDINGS HAVE ONE RENDERING, here, beside the sum and beside the derivation that mints
// them. The adapter used to render two of them ("the one-shot boot override was still pending...", "the
// host was powered off after the attempt without an observed terminal...") from its OWN copies of the
// two decisions; those copies are deleted (review 81601), and a finding the core derives is a finding
// the core renders. The two sentences are unchanged, so a reader of the bundle sees the same text.
fn derived_finding_text(f: DerivedFinding) -> String {
  match f {
    MediaAttachNotReady { record: _ } => "media attach: the presentation was not ready for this attempt"
    MediaAttachSessionHeld { record: _ } => "media attach: the presentation was served only while our own session held it"
    HandoffWithheldAtRecheck { handoff: _ } => "the pre-handoff recheck withheld the handoff, so no boot-device or power write was made"
    HandoffUnconfirmed { handoff: _ } => "the boot-device and power handoff was attempted and not confirmed"
    OverrideNotConsumed { consumption: c } => override_not_consumed_text(c: c)
    // The two clear findings route through the two state renderers beside their sums, so one vocabulary
    // renders one sum (the same rule the override finding above already follows).
    OverrideStaleNotCleared { clearance: c } =>
      concat("an override was already pending before this attempt set one and would not clear (", stale_override_clearance_text(c: c) as String, "), so the attempt refused and nothing was attributed to it")
    OverrideLeftPendingAtRelease { clear: r } =>
      concat("this attempt set a boot override and its release clear did not land (", boot_override_clear_text(c: r) as String, "); the next attempt will find it pending")
    PoweredOffWithoutTerminalMarker => "the host was powered off after the attempt without an observed terminal: the image did not reach its end"
    PresentationAfterHandoff { presentation: p } => concat("after the handoff the presentation was still served: ", presentation_text(p: p))
  }
}

// THE OVERRIDE FINDING'S SENTENCES, in the module that owns the consumption sum. The two the harness
// used to write itself are kept verbatim; the rest route through override_consumption_text so a reader
// sees one vocabulary for one sum.
fn override_not_consumed_text(c: BootOverrideConsumption) -> String {
  match c {
    OverrideStillPendingAfterAttempt => "the one-shot boot override was still pending after the attempt: the host did not boot through it"
    OverridePendingBeforeAttempt => "a boot override matching this attempt's was already pending before it was set, so its consumption cannot be attributed to this attempt"
    _ => override_consumption_text(c: c)
  }
}

// THE ANSWER TO "WHAT WENT WRONG WITH THIS ATTEMPT", kept as the TWO sources it has rather than one
// flattened list. Two sums may not share an arm name in this language, which is a fact of the checker
// and a fact of the design at once: the two lists answer different questions and a single vocabulary
// would let one carry the other's facts. `run_record_findings` is the only way to ask.
type RunRecordFindings {
  standing: List<StandingFinding>,
  derived: List<DerivedFinding>,
}


// ── THE RUN RECORD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE RECORD OF ONE BOOT ATTEMPT, neutral (operator ruling 2026-10-14). It carries the attempt's
// classifications (media, handoff, end media, power-after, override), the attempt's own timing
// instrument, the attempt identity, and the BY-REFERENCE pointer to the adapter's diagnostic plus the
// neutral findings. It does NOT carry: the raw controller readings (they live in the adapter), the
// Ampere SMpro readings (Ampere is a platform capability, cut 2 — snappy-seal-211), or the raw IPMI
// param5 (the override consumption is a classification, correction 6).

type BootRunRecord {
  terminal: TerminalObserved,
  end_media: EndMedia,
  handoff_media: HandoffMedia,
  media: MediaAttachRecord,
  override: BootOverrideConsumption,
  // WHAT THE ROUTE DID ABOUT AN OVERRIDE THAT WAS ALREADY PENDING, and about the one it set itself. Both
  // are standings, not bytes: the bytes stay with the adapter that read them (same rule as `override`
  // above, and the reason the two of them live here rather than being folded into one classification --
  // a stale override that refused the attempt and a release that left the flag pending are DIFFERENT
  // failures, and the boot-5/6 lockout was only visible because they could be told apart).
  override_stale: StaleOverrideClearance,
  override_release: BootOverrideClear,
  power_after: PowerAfterAttempt,
  timing: BootPhaseTiming,
  identity: AttemptIdentityReading,
  diagnostic: DiagnosticRef,
  // Standing facts only (see StandingFinding): what the attempt observed BESIDE these fields. A
  // finding the fields themselves state has no arm here, so it cannot be stored, duplicated, or
  // contradicted. Ask run_record_findings, which returns the standing list it stores and the derived
  // list it computes as the TWO things they are -- it does not flatten them into one vocabulary, so a
  // consumer cannot mistake a stored claim for a computed one or vice versa.
  standing: List<StandingFinding>
}


// ── THE RUN RECORD, AS A FINDINGS LIST AND AS TEXT ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE ONE WAY TO ASK WHAT WENT WRONG. Two sources are concatenated, each with exactly one home:
//
//   record.standing      — stored, because no field states it (which BMC channels failed, whether the
//                          per-attempt credential was removed).
//   derived from fields  — computed here, never stored: a not-ready attach is what `media` says, a
//                          withheld / refused / unconfirmed handoff is what `handoff_media` says, an
//                          override still pending is what `override` says, a powered-off run with no
//                          terminal marker is what `power_after` and `terminal` say together.
//
// Review 81154: these used to be APPENDED to a stored `findings` list, so a caller could store one of
// them and have it reported twice, or store one its own fields contradict. Narrowing the stored field
// to `StandingFinding` makes both unwritable rather than merely wrong -- §5's construction over
// validation, and the same correction the stored `ready` boolean already got.

fn run_record_findings(record: BootRunRecord) -> RunRecordFindings {
  RunRecordFindings {
    standing: record.standing,
    derived: run_record_derived_findings(record: record),
  }
}

// The seven classifications, read out of the record's own fields. Split out so a witness can assert a
// single derived finding without also having to assert the standing list the caller supplied.
fn run_record_derived_findings(record: BootRunRecord) -> List<DerivedFinding> {
  // An attach that was never attempted is not a not-ready attach, and a medium served only under our
  // own closing session is a different fact from one the controller refused to serve: the three are the
  // harness's own three arms (media_attach_harness), restated here from the fields the record holds.
  // This is the ONE match on BootPresentation that classifies, beside presentation_text which renders.
  // A readiness predicate used to sit here too (media_attach_ready), answering "can this boot proceed"
  // by folding session-held and not-served together -- the same fold the stored `ready` boolean got in
  // correction 2, and it had no caller, so both are gone: a finding must keep those arms apart, and a
  // consumer that wants to proceed asks the arms rather than a Bool that flattens them (review 81263 F1,
  // ctrl's predicate-dissolution rule).
  let attach = match record.media {
    MediaAttachNotAttempted { reason: _ } => []
    MediaAttachAttempted {
      medium_image_name: _, medium_image_sha256: _, power: _, stale_power_off: _, presentation: p
    } =>
      match p {
        Presented => []
        PresentationNotServed { reason: _ } => [MediaAttachNotReady { record: record.media }]
        PresentationSessionHeld { detail: _ } => [MediaAttachSessionHeld { record: record.media }]
        // The read never answered, so neither "not ready" nor "session held" is known; the record's own
        // presentation field already says that, and a finding repeating it would be a second home for it.
        PresentationUnread { reason: _ } => []
      }
  }
  let handoff =
    match record.handoff_media {
      HandoffMediaNotReached { reason: _ } => []
      HandoffMediaWithheldAtRecheck { reason: _ } => [HandoffWithheldAtRecheck { handoff: record.handoff_media }]
      // The call result and the after-look are two separate facts of one record, and the harness raises
      // a finding for each: an unconfirmed (or refused) call, and a medium still being served after the
      // handoff. Both are read from the fields here, so a record cannot state one and omit the other.
      HandoffMediaAttempted { handoff: h, after: a } =>
        (match h {
           HandoffConfirmed => []
           HandoffAttemptedUnconfirmed { reason: _ } => [HandoffUnconfirmed { handoff: record.handoff_media }]
         })
        + (match a { Presented => []  _ => [PresentationAfterHandoff { presentation: a }] })
    }
  let override =
    match record.override {
      OverrideConsumedByAttempt => []
      OverrideNoLongerPendingAfterAttempt => []
      OverridePendingBeforeAttempt => [OverrideNotConsumed { consumption: record.override }]
      OverrideStillPendingAfterAttempt => [OverrideNotConsumed { consumption: record.override }]
      OverrideNotHandedOff { reason: _ } => []
      OverrideConsumptionUnread { reason: _ } => []
    }
  // THE TWO CLEARS, read out of the fields the record holds. A stale override that would not clear is the
  // boot-5 refusal (run 40258937016); a release that left the flag pending is the boot-6 ending (run
  // 40264233543) -- each raises its own finding because each is a different thing to fix. An UNREAD clear
  // raises the release finding too: "we do not know the override was cleared" is the same exposure to the
  // NEXT attempt as "it is still pending", and the release is the only thing standing between them.
  let stale =
    match record.override_stale {
      StaleOverrideNoneFound => []
      StaleOverrideCleared => []
      StaleOverrideWouldNotClear => [OverrideStaleNotCleared { clearance: record.override_stale }]
      StaleOverrideUnread { reason: _ } => []
    }
  let released =
    match record.override_release {
      OverrideClearNotRequired { reason: _ } => []
      OverrideCleared => []
      OverrideClearLeftPending => [OverrideLeftPendingAtRelease { clear: record.override_release }]
      OverrideClearUnread { reason: _ } => [OverrideLeftPendingAtRelease { clear: record.override_release }]
    }
  let power =
    match record.power_after {
      PoweredOffWithoutTerminal => [PoweredOffWithoutTerminalMarker]
      _ => []
    }
  attach + handoff + override + stale + released + power
}

// THE RUN RECORD, RENDERED. The run section's text, one line per classification, in the order the
// harness reads it.


// ── THE NEUTRAL BUDGET (correction 5: only the publish allowance is core; the Redfish
// read budgets belong to the adapter; the console bound moved with its block to
// gunbc.host.boot_console_observation console_bound) ─────────────────────────────
// The allowance for the medium publish (the time the medium image is written to the medium before the
// attach is read back as served). A policy bound, not a mtcollins1 measurement. It carries its unit in
// its type -- the same carrier the row's `publish_allowance` field and the moved `console_bound`
// (gunbc.host.boot_console_observation) use -- so a second cannot be mistaken for a millisecond or a count at any consumer. A row that runs
// with this default adopts it; the value has one authority (review 81074).

data publish_allowance: Second = second(count: 60)


// THE DEBT, ON A CARRIER A PROGRAM CAN READ (review 81187, DESIGN §5: "a dissolution condition
// describes how admitted debt ends; it does not authorize creating it"). The header paragraph says in
// prose what this row says in data, and the prose is not what the debt is tracked by: this is a
// DissolutionCondition, so std.dissolution answers "is the console debt still live?" from the
// declaration census without anyone rereading a comment.
//
// THE SHAPE IS DELIBERATE: a retirement trigger, not a forward one. The forward shape would name
// gunbc.host.boot_console_observation as where the declarations live -- and while that re-home was only
// planned, naming it would have been a promise about a name someone may still choose not to use, the
// fabricated-citation class a runner spec refuses by name (std.dissolution's header says the same, and
// says the retirement trigger was missing for want of an author, not for want of a reason). A
// retirement trigger instead names where a declaration was DECLARED when the debt was admitted, so the
// condition is verifiable: THE DEBT IS NOW DISCHARGED. The re-home landed (4d-ii,
// node://adhoc-18598508-4f4) -- ConsoleReading is no longer declared here, the two machine-named modules
// no longer declare the five borrowings, and every successor is declared at
// gunbc.host.boot_console_observation -- so the census lacks all six items and the fold below reads
// DissolutionFired. The refs list keeps naming the RETIRED homes on purpose: that is what a retirement
// trigger is, and deleting it would erase the debt's history rather than discharge it (a discharged
// debt still says what was paid).
data boot_attempt_console_reading_decl_ref: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.host.boot_attempt",
  decl_name: "ConsoleReading",
)

// THE WHOLE DEBT, AS DATA (reviews 81263 F3, 85810): SIX declarations, ONE population -- all RETIRED.
// Five were borrowed from two machine-named modules (gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_power_on_account
// and gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_console_milestone_observation) and one was the local
// ConsoleReading the split retires; the 4d-ii re-home retired all six into
// gunbc.host.boot_console_observation. Naming only ConsoleReading (as the carrier did first)
// under-reported the debt -- and a carrier whose status question reads only part of the population is
// the same fact with two answers, the exact shape review 85810 rejected. So the status fold below reads
// THIS list and nothing else (single source): the debt is discharged when the census lacks every item,
// and which specific borrowing is left is readable without anyone rereading a comment. Today the census
// lacks every item, so the fold answers Fired.
data boot_attempt_console_debt_refs: List<DeclarationRef> = [
  boot_attempt_console_reading_decl_ref,
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_power_on_account", decl_name: "SpanConsoleReading"),
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_power_on_account", decl_name: "span_console_readings"),
  decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_console_milestone_observation",
    decl_name: "ConsoleMilestoneObservation",
  ),
  decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_console_milestone_observation",
    decl_name: "mtcollins1_console_milestones",
  ),
  decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_console_milestone_observation",
    decl_name: "console_milestone_sighting_text",
  ),
]

fn boot_attempt_console_debt_condition(ref: DeclarationRef) -> DissolutionCondition {
  retires_dissolution(ref: ref)
}

// WHICH BORROWINGS ARE STILL LIVE, given the declaration census. An empty answer is the debt being gone.
fn boot_attempt_console_debt_outstanding(present_decls: List<DeclarationRef>) -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  filter(boot_attempt_console_debt_refs, ref => match dissolution_status(
    condition: boot_attempt_console_debt_condition(ref: ref),
    present_decls: present_decls,
  ) {
    DissolutionPending => true
    DissolutionFired => false
    // Unbound (only reachable for an UnboundDissolution condition, which none of these are) counts as
    // OUTSTANDING: a condition nobody wired up is not evidence the debt is gone. Failing closed here means
    // a mistyped carrier keeps the debt visible instead of silently discharging it (§5, "a failure arm
    // refuses, never widens").
    DissolutionUnbound => true
  })
}

// THE ONE QUESTION THE CARRIER ANSWERS, as a FOLD over the outstanding list (review 85810): the debt is
// discharged exactly when nothing borrowed is still present, so "is it gone?" has ONE source -- the
// outstanding fold -- and cannot disagree with it. An answer keyed to one declaration only (as the first
// carrier did, naming ConsoleReading while five mtcollins1_ borrowings sat beside it) is the same fact
// with two writers, which is exactly what this fold removes. CONSUMERS today: the mapping witness
// (megarac_boot_record_mapping_witness) -- claims (b)/(c)/(c2) exercise the fold's LOGIC on a census the
// witness assembles, and claim (d) feeds it a population BUILT FROM A CORPUS READ (v2.std.decl_index
// decl_facts_at), which is what review 86612 required: a fold nobody hands the real corpus to is a lens
// nothing gates on (§6). At dissolution, the re-home that retires these six declarations (work item
// node://adhoc-de73c7a1-87b) flips this to Fired by making the census lack them -- no other code path may
// assert the debt is gone. CAVEAT carried from std.dissolution's header:
// an empty census reports FIRED, which is "nothing is present" and not a default -- a caller must hand
// over the real declaration population or it is answering about a corpus it never read.
fn boot_attempt_console_debt_status(present_decls: List<DeclarationRef>) -> DissolutionStatus {
  if length(xs: boot_attempt_console_debt_outstanding(present_decls: present_decls)) == 0 {
    DissolutionFired
  } else {
    DissolutionPending
  }
}
